---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
import { DRUG_BASE, drugBaseNote } from '../lib/medications';

/* R296 [§S7]: the spend bar's labels and the widths that encode them are the
   same two numbers, read once from the model's own decomposition. The bar used
   to be a hand-typed 65/35 split labelled with a CMS figure that is not the
   model's retail component. */
const retail = DRUG_BASE.retail.toFixed(0);
const nonRetail = DRUG_BASE.nonRetail.toFixed(0);
const drugBase = DRUG_BASE.total.toFixed(1);
const retailWidth = DRUG_BASE.retailPct.toFixed(2);
const nonRetailWidth = DRUG_BASE.nonRetailPct.toFixed(2);
const retail2023 = DRUG_BASE.retail2023.toFixed(1);
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Medications">
  <main>

  <section class="card chapter-intro">
    <span class="chapter-intro-eyebrow">Chapter 6 · Medicines</span>
    <h2>How can one public purchaser lower prices and keep essential drugs available?</h2>
    <p class="desc">National purchasing covers the complete formulary. The
      Public Medicines Corporation, or PMC, does something narrower: it
      creates permanent public capacity for 200 essential, shortage-prone,
      high-volume, and preparedness product families. This chapter separates
      those two levers and shows where each one applies.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card medications-hero">
    <span class="medications-kicker">A public option on the supply side</span>
    <h2>The PMC is bargaining power, backup capacity, and a manufacturer of last resort</h2>
    <p class="desc">A national buyer can negotiate only when it has legal
      authority, credible alternatives, and the ability to walk away without
      interrupting care. The PMC supplies that alternative for a controlled
      portfolio. It does not replace the complete private drug industry or
      relax FDA quality requirements.</p>
    <div class="medications-hero-stats">
      <article>
        <strong>200</strong>
        <span>controlled product families</span>
        <small>specified portfolio <span class="conf high">high</span></small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>61 → 200</strong>
        <span>families qualified from Phase 5 to Phase 8</span>
        <small>staged obligation <span class="conf high">high</span></small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>$43B/yr</strong>
        <span>illustrative PMC-attributed purchase saving</span>
        <small>modal planning midpoint <span class="conf low">low</span></small>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="medications-kicker">Why public capacity is needed</span>
    <h2>The market can make a medicine cheap and still make its supply fragile</h2>
    <p class="desc">Generic competition lowers prices when several qualified
      suppliers remain. It can fail when low margins drive producers away,
      one quality problem idles a scarce sterile line, or a buyer cannot see
      inventory across the supply chain. FDA identifies manufacturing quality
      problems as the most common reason for shortages and cannot require a
      company to continue or expand production.</p>
    <div class="medications-need-grid">
      <article>
        <span>Continuity</span>
        <h3>Keep indispensable products in production</h3>
        <p>Standing public lines do not abandon older injectables, antidotes,
          or small markets solely because another product is more profitable.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Competition</span>
        <h3>Create a credible cost-plus alternative</h3>
        <p>A qualified public product gives the national purchaser a real
          fallback when a supplier rejects reasonable price or access terms.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Visibility</span>
        <h3>Connect demand, inventory, lots, and capacity</h3>
        <p>The pharmacy utility and national inventory view reveal shortages
          early enough to shift orders, release stock, or add production.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Preparedness</span>
        <h3>Maintain surge capability before an emergency</h3>
        <p>Antidotes, fluids, antibiotics, and other response products need
          validated processes and qualified inputs before demand spikes.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-shortages/frequently-asked-questions-about-drug-shortages"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA: causes and limits of shortage response</a>;
      <a href="https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/impacts-nonprofit-membership-based-pharmaceutical-company"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">HHS: nonprofit manufacturing precedent</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="medications-kicker">The current bill</span>
    <h2>Retail spending is the visible line, not the complete medication cost</h2>
    <div class="medications-cost-layout">
      <div class="medications-cost-stack">
        <article>
          <strong>${retail2023}B</strong>
          <span>retail prescription drugs in 2023</span>
          <small>Official CMS account for the last finalized year, and the
            line the model calibrates on
            <span class="conf high">high</span></small>
        </article>
        <article>
          <strong>$680–730B</strong>
          <span>retail plus hospital and clinician-administered drugs in 2024</span>
          <small>Derived because CMS embeds non-retail drugs in service lines
            <span class="conf medium">medium</span></small>
        </article>
        <article>
          <strong>2.78×</strong>
          <span>U.S. prices relative to 33 comparison countries</span>
          <small>2022 all-drug price ratio; brand-name ratio was 4.22×
            <span class="conf high">high</span></small>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="medications-cost-explainer">
        <h3>Why the all-drug total is a range</h3>
        <p>CMS reports medicines dispensed by retail and mail-order pharmacies
          as a separate category. Infused, injected, and other
          clinician-administered medicines are recorded inside hospital and
          physician-service spending. The fiscal model adds an estimate of that
          second group to the retail line and states the total in real 2024
          dollars: ${retail}B plus ${nonRetail}B is a ${drugBase}B drug base,
          inside the derived range.</p>
        <div class="medications-spend-bar" aria-label="Modeled all-drug spending split">
          <span style={`width:${retailWidth}%`}>${retail}B modeled retail</span>
          <span style={`width:${nonRetailWidth}%`}>${nonRetail}B modeled non-retail</span>
        </div>
        <p class="note">Both segments are the model's own. The retail segment
          is the 2023 CMS line expressed in real 2024 dollars, which is not the
          same quantity as a CMS total for 2024: this site calibrates every
          figure on the last finalized CMS year and reports results in real
          2024 dollars. Published 2024 retail figures for this line do not
          currently reconcile with the 2023 account the model uses, so the
          model does not carry one.</p>
        <p class="note">The international ratio is evidence of price headroom,
          not a promise that every U.S. price can fall to the comparison
          average. U.S. generic prices were already lower in the RAND study.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://www.cms.gov/data-research/statistics-trends-and-reports/national-health-expenditure-data/nhe-fact-sheet"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS 2024 National Health Expenditures</a>;
      <a href="https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_reports/RRA788-3.html"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">RAND international price comparison</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="medications-kicker">Why negotiation is narrow today</span>
    <h2>Medicare can negotiate now, but only inside a defined statutory lane</h2>
    <p class="desc">The Part D noninterference law still bars the HHS
      Secretary from taking over negotiations among manufacturers, pharmacies,
      and drug plans; requiring a particular formulary; or imposing a Part D
      price structure outside the negotiation exception. Medicare also pays
      plans and providers rather than directly buying the nation's complete
      drug supply.</p>
    <div class="medications-law-flow" aria-label="Current Medicare negotiation limits">
      <article>
        <span>1 · Screen</span>
        <strong>High expenditure</strong>
        <p>CMS ranks Medicare spending, not the complete U.S. market.</p>
      </article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
      <article>
        <span>2 · Wait</span>
        <strong>7 years for drugs, 11 for biologics</strong>
        <p>The age floors run from FDA approval or licensure to selection.</p>
      </article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
      <article>
        <span>3 · Exclude competition</span>
        <strong>Single source only</strong>
        <p>A marketed generic or biosimilar generally removes the product
          from this negotiation pathway.</p>
      </article>
      <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
      <article>
        <span>4 · Phase in</span>
        <strong>10, then 15, then 15</strong>
        <p>Prices apply in 2026, 2027, and 2028 for the first three cycles.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="medications-negotiation-proof">
      <strong>$12B</strong>
      <div>
        <h3>The narrow lane still demonstrates bargaining power</h3>
        <p>CMS estimates the 15 prices effective in 2027 would have reduced
          2024 net spending on those medicines by about $12B, or 44%, before
          the Coverage Gap Discount Program adjustment. Those medicines were
          about 15% of Part D gross drug costs, not the national formulary.</p>
        <span class="conf high">high confidence</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/42/1395w-111"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">42 U.S.C. 1395w-111(i)</a>;
      <a href="https://www.cms.gov/initiatives/medicare-prescription-drug-affordability/overview/medicare-drug-price-negotiation-program/selected-drugs-negotiated-prices"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS selected drugs and negotiated prices</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="medications-kicker">The complete public-production portfolio</span>
    <h2>Search all 200 product families</h2>
    <p class="desc">A family identifies the medicine and the essential forms
      the public line must be able to make. It is not permission to market a
      product. Exact strengths, labeling, intellectual-property route, FDA
      application, facility, process, and lot release must still qualify.</p>
    <div class="medications-family-controls">
      <label>
        Search medicine, form, or reason
        <input id="medications-family-search" type="search"
          placeholder="Try insulin, sterile, shortage, or maternal">
      </label>
      <label>
        Qualification phase
        <select id="medications-phase-filter">
          <option value="">All phases</option>
          <option value="P5">Phase 5 · Year 7</option>
          <option value="P6">Phase 6 · Year 8</option>
          <option value="P7">Phase 7 · Year 10</option>
          <option value="P8">Phase 8 · Year 12</option>
        </select>
      </label>
      <label>
        Why included
        <select id="medications-reason-filter">
          <option value="">All reasons</option>
          <option value="critical">Immediate clinical need</option>
          <option value="high volume">High routine volume</option>
          <option value="shortage risk">Shortage or concentration risk</option>
          <option value="price and equity">Price or access inequity</option>
          <option value="maternal and child">Maternal and child need</option>
          <option value="preparedness">Emergency preparedness</option>
        </select>
      </label>
    </div>
    <div class="medications-family-summary">
      <strong id="medications-family-count">200 of 200 product families</strong>
      <span>One family can satisfy several inclusion reasons.</span>
    </div>
    <div id="medications-family-list" class="medications-family-list"
      aria-live="polite"></div>
    <p class="note">The portfolio deliberately includes conventional oral
      generics, complex sterile injectables, inhaled products, selected
      biologics, insulin, oncology drugs, maternal and neonatal medicines,
      antidotes, and emergency-response products. Manufacturing difficulty
      determines when a family qualifies, not whether it matters.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="medications-kicker">How government production works</span>
    <h2>Public ownership changes the mission, not the scientific standard</h2>
    <div class="medications-production-flow">
      <article>
        <span>1</span>
        <h3>Specify</h3>
        <p>Choose the exact strength, form, demand forecast, inventory horizon,
          and clinical substitution rules.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>2</span>
        <h3>Clear rights</h3>
        <p>Use expired patents, licenses, public-interest terms, or lawful
          federal use with compensation when authorized.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>3</span>
        <h3>Secure inputs</h3>
        <p>Qualify active ingredients, containers, critical components, and a
          separate backup source.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>4</span>
        <h3>Control the line</h3>
        <p>Build or acquire a PMC facility, or control an affiliate or contract
          line under the same public duties.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>5</span>
        <h3>Win FDA approval</h3>
        <p>Submit the appropriate generic, biologic, or new-drug application;
          pass review, inspection, and process validation.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>6</span>
        <h3>Release and monitor</h3>
        <p>Test every lot, publish source and capacity status, monitor safety,
          retain inventory, and exercise recall procedures.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="medications-quality-boundary">
      <strong>FDA remains the regulator</strong>
      <p>The PMC would submit applications and meet current good manufacturing
        practice like another sponsor. FDA reviews whether the facility,
        equipment, process, controls, and product can consistently meet the
        approved standard.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/types-applications/abbreviated-new-drug-application-anda"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA generic application pathway</a>;
      <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/pharmaceutical-quality-resources/current-good-manufacturing-practice-cgmp-regulations"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA current good manufacturing practice</a>;
      <a href="https://www.gao.gov/products/b-195193"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">GAO explanation of federal patent use</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="medications-kicker">Production timeline</span>
    <h2>Start with authority and proof, then qualify platform by platform</h2>
    <div class="medications-timeline">
      <article>
        <span>Years 1–2 · Phases 0–1</span>
        <strong>Authorize and design</strong>
        <p>Establish the PMC, select presentations, map patents and licenses,
          forecast demand, choose sites, and prepare FDA submissions.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Year 3 · Phase 2</span>
        <strong>First operation</strong>
        <p>Launch national pharmacy purchasing and claims, qualify suppliers,
          exercise shortage controls, and begin initial PMC production.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Year 7 · Phase 5</span>
        <strong>61 families</strong>
        <p>Qualify the first broad oral and routine-care portfolio while
          preserving separate supplier redundancy.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Year 8 · Phase 6</span>
        <strong>177 cumulative</strong>
        <p>Add 116 families, dominated by sterile injectables, acute-care
          products, oncology medicines, fluids, and antidotes.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Year 10 · Phase 7</span>
        <strong>188 cumulative</strong>
        <p>Add 11 device-dependent or complex presentations, including
          inhalers, autoinjectors, and long-acting products.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Year 12 · Phase 8</span>
        <strong>200 cumulative</strong>
        <p>Qualify the final 12 biologic and complex families, certify routine
          output, dual sourcing, inventory visibility, and lifecycle review.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Dates are qualification anchors, not automatic releases.
      A family moves into routine production only after its legal,
      manufacturing, FDA, quality, and supply evidence is complete.
      <span class="conf high">specified schedule</span></p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="medications-kicker">Savings without double counting</span>
    <h2>Attribute part of the national price lever to products the PMC supplies</h2>
    <p class="desc">The fiscal model applies a 25–55% mature net price
      reduction to the complete ${drugBase}B drug base through national
      purchasing, reference pricing, formulary management, and public
      production together. This calculator identifies how much of that same
      saving could be associated with the PMC portfolio. It does not add a new
      offset.</p>
    <p class="note">{drugBaseNote()}</p>
    <div class="medications-savings-layout">
      <div class="medications-savings-controls">
        <label>
          <span>Share of national drug spending supplied by PMC
            <output id="medications-share-value">15%</output></span>
          <input id="medications-share" type="range" min="5" max="25"
            step="1" value="15">
          <small>5–25% planning range; portfolio claims share is not yet observed
            <span class="conf low">low</span></small>
        </label>
        <label>
          <span>Net acquisition-cost reduction on PMC products
            <output id="medications-reduction-value">40%</output></span>
          <input id="medications-reduction" type="range" min="25" max="55"
            step="1" value="40">
          <small>Same range as the fiscal model's national drug-price parameter
            <span class="conf medium">medium</span></small>
        </label>
        <p id="medications-savings-formula" class="medications-savings-formula">
          ${drugBase}B × 15% × 40% = $43.1B per year</p>
      </div>
      <div class="medications-savings-result">
        <span>PMC-attributed annual purchase saving</span>
        <strong id="medications-pmc-savings">$43.1B</strong>
        <small>Illustrative 2024-scale midpoint; stress boundary is
          approximately $9–99B per year. <span class="conf low">low</span></small>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="medications-savings-ledger">
      <div class="medications-savings-track" aria-hidden="true">
        <span id="medications-pmc-share-bar" style="width:15%"></span>
      </div>
      <article>
        <span>PMC-attributed slice</span>
        <strong id="medications-pmc-savings-copy">$43.1B</strong>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Remaining national purchasing and formulary slice</span>
        <strong id="medications-purchasing-savings">$244B</strong>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Complete modeled drug-price lever</span>
        <strong id="medications-total-drug-savings">$287B</strong>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="note">The saving lowers the public system's cost and therefore
      the financing Americans must provide. Covered patients pay $0 at the
      pharmacy, so this is not a separate checkout discount. Start-up capital,
      shortage avoidance, adherence, and health outcomes are outside this
      attribution.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="medications-kicker">What the government does not produce</span>
    <h2>The national formulary is much larger than the public-production portfolio</h2>
    <p class="desc">Medicines outside the 200 families are still covered,
      evaluated, purchased, and monitored. The default tool is national
      purchasing, not a public factory for every product.</p>
    <div class="medications-exclusions">
      <article>
        <h3>Patented single-source products without a cleared rights path</h3>
        <p>Negotiate or license them. Public production waits for an expired
          patent, a voluntary license, public-interest terms, or a lawful
          federal-use decision with compensation.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Patient-specific cell and gene therapies</h3>
        <p>Use qualified specialist centers and outcome-linked purchasing when
          production is inseparable from collection, engineering, and
          reinfusion for one patient.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Very low-volume products with a reliable access contract</h3>
        <p>Purchase from a qualified supplier when a guaranteed contract is
          safer and less costly than maintaining a standing public line.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Products in a resilient, low-price, multi-supplier market</h3>
        <p>Keep purchasing competitively when another manufacturing line would
          add little price discipline or continuity value.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Noncovered cosmetic or non-medically-necessary products</h3>
        <p>Public capacity follows the covered benefit and preparedness need,
          not every product sold in the United States.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Any product that has not completed FDA qualification</h3>
        <p>Portfolio status never substitutes for an approved application,
          inspected facility, validated process, conforming lot, and ongoing
          safety controls.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="note">These are operating categories, not permanent exclusions.
      A product can move into public production when price, shortage,
      preparedness, or equity evidence changes and every legal and FDA
      requirement is satisfied.</p>
    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/medications_methodology.md"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sources and medication savings methodology</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

    <ChapterNav />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/medications-client.ts';
</script>
